Healthcare Digest - In the News
Executive Summary
CMS offered states a hold-harmless period through June 30, 2027, delaying Med=
icaid work-requirement coverage losses while preserving eligibility assessmen=
ts and systems implementation. (
CMS,
Optional Hold Harmless Period for Community Engagement Implementation
, November 13, 2026
)
HealthSpan raised its Surgery Partners offer to $25 per share and accepted st=
ronger closing obligations after a competing proposal, increasing both the va=
lue and closing probability of the national ambulatory-surgery platform. (
SEC,
Surgery Partners Form 8-K
, November 9, 2026
)
CMS extended the nationwide hospice and home-health enrollment moratoria for =
six months but allowed enrolled operators to add locations, shifting the poli=
cy=E2=80=99s competitive advantage toward incumbent expansion.
Federal prior-authorization policy moved from technical specifications to pro=
duction-like workflow testing, creating near-term integration work for payers=
, EHRs and intermediaries without providing a compliance safe harbor. (
ONC,
ONC and CMS Launch Industry Electronic Prior Authorization Testing Initiative
, November 12, 2026
)
Overall environment: modestly improved for payers, providers and health techn=
ology because Medicaid coverage disruption was deferred and implementation de=
mand increased, although entry restrictions and P3=E2=80=99s financing exposu=
re show continued pressure on smaller operators.
1. CMS turns first-half 2027 Medicaid work checks into a shadow eligibility p=
eriod
What happened
States may suspend Medicaid eligibility denials and disenrollments tied solel=
y to community-engagement noncompliance through June 30, 2027. They must stil=
l assess qualifying activities, deemed compliance and exclusions for relevant=
 applications and renewals beginning January 1. CMS will exclude cases handle=
d under an approved mitigation plan from PERM, Medicaid Eligibility Quality C=
ontrol and other beneficiary-eligibility audit errors. States seeking the opt=
ion from January 1 should notify CMS by December 11. (
CMS,
Optional Hold Harmless Period for Community Engagement Implementation
, November 13, 2026
)
Implication
The policy defers the first wave of work-related coverage losses, supporting =
Medicaid MCO membership and reducing near-term uncompensated-care exposure fo=
r providers. It does not reduce implementation scope. States still need data =
matching, exclusion logic, notices, caseworker workflows and auditable eligib=
ility decisions.
January through June becomes a production testing period in states adopting t=
he option. Eligibility vendors gain additional time to correct system defects=
, while MCOs and providers retain more opportunity to intervene before advers=
e determinations. Variation will remain high because states can decline the o=
ption or use it for a shorter period.
Watch next
State mitigation-plan decisions by December 11 and whether CMS litigation or =
additional guidance changes the July 1 enforcement threshold.
2. A rival bid raises the price and closing obligations for Surgery Partners
What happened
HealthSpan Partners increased its offer for Surgery Partners to $25 per share=
, lifting enterprise value from approximately $5.7 billion to as much as $6.0=
 billion after Surgery Partners received an unsolicited competing proposal. T=
he amendment adds a 10% annualized ticking fee after March 1, 2027, capped at=
 $1.61 per share, and removes the materiality limit from HealthSpan=E2=80=99s=
 obligation to take actions needed to resolve antitrust impediments. The term=
ination fee increased to approximately $149.1 million. (
SEC,
Surgery Partners Form 8-K
, November 9, 2026
)
Surgery Partners also reported 6.0% same-facility revenue growth, driven by 5=
.8% higher revenue per case and only 0.2% case growth. (
SEC,
Surgery Partners Third Quarter 2026 Results
, November 9, 2026
)
Implication
The amendment strengthens last week=E2=80=99s thesis that scaled ASC networks=
 have become scarce strategic assets for health systems. Competing interest r=
aised the purchase price despite limited underlying volume growth, indicating=
 that geographic reach, physician relationships and site-of-care positioning =
carry substantial option value.
The expanded regulatory covenant improves closing certainty but may require H=
ealthSpan to accept divestitures or operating restrictions. Payers should ass=
ess concentration and reimbursement exposure in markets where Surgery Partner=
s overlaps with Bon Secours Mercy Health.
Watch next
The identity of the rival bidder, the proxy filing and any antitrust remedy a=
ffecting local ASC ownership or contracting.
3. CMS extends the home-health and hospice entry freeze while letting incumbe=
nts add locations
What happened
CMS extended nationwide moratoria on initial Medicare enrollment of home-heal=
th agencies and hospices, including certain changes in majority ownership, fo=
r another six months. Unlike the May moratoria, currently enrolled organizati=
ons may now add practice locations. CMS said an October California operation =
produced 145 revocations involving providers associated with more than $703 m=
illion in Medicare and Medicaid payments. (
CMS,
CMS Extends and Modifies Nationwide Freeze on New Medicare Hospice and Home H=
ealth Enrollment
, November 12, 2026
;
Federal Register,
Extension and Modification of the Nationwide Temporary Moratorium on Enrollme=
nt of Hospices
, November 16, 2026
)
Implication
The extension adds a market-structure effect to last week=E2=80=99s retroacti=
ve-revocation policy. Greenfield Medicare entry and some control transactions=
 remain blocked, while established operators can expand through additional lo=
cations. Scaled incumbents therefore gain a six-month advantage in recruiting=
 clinicians, entering adjacent service areas and negotiating payer relationsh=
ips.
The ownership restriction may also complicate acquisitions structured around =
new billing enrollments or majority-control changes. Diligence must address e=
nrollment continuity, managing personnel and whether a transaction falls with=
in the moratorium rather than treating provider numbers as routine closing it=
ems.
Watch next
Access indicators in rural markets, further payment suspensions and whether C=
MS extends or narrows the moratoria again in May 2027.
4. Federal prior-authorization testing shifts the risk from APIs to end-to-en=
d workflow
What happened
ONC and CMS launched voluntary 10- to 12-week testing sprints for payers, pro=
viders, EHR developers and intermediaries using production-like environments.=
 Testing will cover the Da Vinci Coverage Requirements Discovery, Documentati=
on Templates and Rules, and Prior Authorization Support implementation guides=
. MITRE will support recruitment and coordination, while Drummond Group will =
operate the FHIRplace testing environment. Organizations must express interes=
t by November 20; participation does not constitute certification or a CMS co=
mpliance determination. (
ONC,
ONC and CMS Launch Industry Electronic Prior Authorization Testing Initiative
, November 12, 2026
)
Implication
The initiative recognizes that standards conformance does not ensure an opera=
tional authorization. The remaining failure points sit between organizations:=
 coverage-rule discovery, clinical-document generation, identity matching, at=
tachments, exception handling and status reconciliation.
That increases demand for workflow orchestration, test data, monitoring and i=
mplementation services around payer and EHR APIs. Clearinghouses and interope=
rability vendors can capture work that neither payer cores nor EHRs manage al=
one. Voluntary testing may reduce deployment risk, but organizations remain r=
esponsible for compliance and cannot treat participation as a safe harbor.
Watch next
The first-round participants, selected clinical use cases and whether sprint =
findings become formal implementation guidance or change enforcement expectat=
ions.
5. P3=E2=80=99s margin recovery still leaves a financing problem
What happened
P3 Health reported third-quarter adjusted EBITDA of $20.9 million versus a $4=
5.9 million loss a year earlier. Medical margin reached $43.9 million, or $14=
0 per member per month, but fell to $35.0 million and $111 PMPM after excludi=
ng payer settlements and prior-year development. At-risk membership declined =
9% to approximately 105,000 following network and payer rationalization. (
SEC,
P3 Health Partners Third Quarter 2026 Results
, November 9, 2026
)
P3 nevertheless reported $43.9 million of unrestricted cash, a $190.6 million=
 working-capital deficit and $90.1 million of nine-month operating cash use, =
concluding that substantial doubt remains about its ability to continue as a =
going concern. (
SEC,
P3 Health Partners Form 10-Q
, November 9, 2026
)
Implication
The result strengthens the case that selective payer and provider contraction=
 can repair value-based-care unit economics. It weakens the assumption that p=
ositive adjusted EBITDA resolves the sector=E2=80=99s capital problem. Settle=
ment timing, preferred obligations, debt maturities and claims funding can st=
ill overwhelm reported operating improvement.
Health-plan and physician partners should treat liquidity as a service-contin=
uity and counterparty risk. Smaller enablement platforms may need refinancing=
, asset sales or strategic capital before their improved medical margins conv=
ert into sustainable cash generation.
Watch next
P3=E2=80=99s capital-raising or refinancing plan and recurring 2027 medical m=
argin after prior-period benefits fall away.
What to Watch For
November 20, 2026: Organizations must express interest in the first ONC-CMS e=
lectronic prior-authorization testing round. (
ONC,
ONC and CMS Launch Industry Electronic Prior Authorization Testing Initiative
, November 12, 2026
)
December 7, 2026: Medicare annual enrollment ends, providing the first firm m=
embership read after 2027 benefit and Stars changes. (
CMS,
Medicare Advantage and Medicare Prescription Drug Programs Expected to Remain=
 Stable in 2027
, October 2026
)
December 11, 2026: States intending to use the Medicaid work-requirement hold=
-harmless option from January 1 are asked to notify CMS. (
CMS,
Optional Hold Harmless Period for Community Engagement Implementation
, November 13, 2026
)
December 15, 2026: Comments close on CMS=E2=80=99s proposed MA provider-direc=
tory and Part D PBM accountability rule. (
Federal Register,
CMS-4214-P
, October 20, 2026
)
Bottom Line
CMS=E2=80=99s Medicaid hold-harmless option is the week=E2=80=99s most import=
ant economic change because it defers first-half 2027 coverage losses while p=
reserving the full eligibility-technology build. State implementation choices=
 and resulting MCO membership variation need the closest attention. The Decem=
ber 11 notification date is the next event most likely to determine how much =
coverage and provider-cash exposure moves from January into the second half o=
f 2027.
